# Mutation cases for "the rewriter is retired, not merely second".
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/the_rewriter_is_retired_not_merely_second.cases`.
#
# `docs/SCE_LUA_TRANSLATION_SEAM.md` records two halves of one decision. The
# first — LINK `sce-build`'s ECMAScript frontend beside `lua54` — landed with
# its own row and its own check, and it was true for a day while every
# expression the frontend refused still went to `EcmaScriptToLuaTransformer`.
# The document said so itself: *"An empty list is not a retired rewriter …
# retiring the rewriter is the claim that nothing takes the fallback at all,
# and that needs its own witness rather than this one's silence."*
#
# The `retire-rewriter` row is that witness and `retirement:rewriter-deleted`
# is what re-derives it. The unit is now GONE — header, source and the
# benchmark that priced it — so the row's claim moved from "nothing calls it"
# to "it is not here, and nothing could".
#
# ⚠ Deleting the unit deleted the sweep's positive control. While it existed,
# the predicate that reports zero had to report the unit's OWN files or it had
# stopped being able to see the name at all. "No file names X" is also what a
# sweep answers when it read nothing, so the control had to be bought back:
# the tree still explains the rewriter in comments, and the check requires
# those raw mentions to stay above a floor. The cases below aim at that seam
# more than at anything else, because it is the one that can go quietly blind.
#
# ⚠⚠⚠ THE NAME EXISTED TWICE, and this file covers both retirements. C++'s
# closed on 2026-08-29 and Kotlin's on 2026-08-30, against two populations with
# two sets of call sites, so `retirement:rewriter-deleted` and
# `retirement:kotlin-rewriter-deleted` are two rows and the cases below come in
# two groups. Folding them into one sweep over "every tracked source" is the
# economy to refuse: it would have reported the Kotlin half retired from the
# day C++ deleted its own, four rounds before any Kotlin file stopped reaching
# the rewriter — the invisibility failure this file's C++ half already paid for
# once, in a directory boundary.
#
# ⚠⚠ Two measurements retired WITH the subject rather than with the code:
# `per-call-cost` timed the rewriter against the frontend, `swap-net-footprint`
# weighed its object file. Neither can be re-measured here, so both carry a
# `retired-measurement:` pin — a commit that must still hold every departed
# file while this tree holds none of them, plus the probe, kept and marked.
# Three cases below aim at that check, one per way the pin can go false.

mutation_tests -p sce-build --test lowering_decision_ledger
mutation_targets sce-build/tests/lowering_decision_ledger.rs
# Read at run time, not compiled in: the gate opens the tree's C++ sources,
# the ledger document, a CMake list and the retired probes, so a change to
# any of them is invisible to a rebuild check.
mutation_runtime_targets sce/include/scripting/LuaEngine.h
mutation_runtime_targets sce/src/scripting/LuaEngine.cpp
mutation_runtime_targets tests/engine/LoweredEcma262Test.cpp
mutation_runtime_targets sce/sce_base_sources.cmake
mutation_runtime_targets docs/SCE_LUA_TRANSLATION_SEAM.md
mutation_runtime_targets scripts/measure-lowering-per-call.sh
mutation_runtime_targets .github/workflows/tree-hygiene.yml
mutation_runtime_targets scripts/gates/tree-hygiene.sh
# The Kotlin half's own subjects, added when `kotlin-retire-rewriter` closed.
mutation_runtime_targets backends/kotlin/lua/src/main/kotlin/com/sce/scripting/lua/LuaScriptEngine.kt
mutation_runtime_targets backends/kotlin/tests/src/test/kotlin/com/sce/ecmascript/LoweredEcma262Test.kt

# --- the subject: a caller comes back -------------------------------------
#
# Written as a REVERT, not as an invention. The member and the include are how
# the engine actually stood before the retirement, so a careless revert — the
# shape this repository has already paid for once, when a call was deleted and
# the paragraph explaining it left standing — is what they reproduce. With the
# unit deleted these no longer even compile, which is the point: the row's
# failure is a build break as well as a false claim.

mutation_case "the engine takes the rewriter back as a member" <<'PY'
edit("sce/include/scripting/LuaEngine.h",
     "    // Engine state\n",
     "    // Expression transformer\n"
     "    EcmaScriptToLuaTransformer transformer_;\n"
     "\n"
     "    // Engine state\n")
PY

mutation_case "the engine includes the retired header again" <<'PY'
edit("sce/src/scripting/LuaEngine.cpp",
     '#include "scripting/LuaDOMBinding.h"',
     '#include "scripting/EcmaScriptToLuaTransformer.h"\n'
     '#include "scripting/LuaDOMBinding.h"')
PY

mutation_case "a caller appears where the old boundary would have hidden it" <<'PY'
edit("tests/engine/LoweredEcma262Test.cpp",
     '#include "scripting/ScriptEngineProvider.h"',
     '#include "scripting/EcmaScriptToLuaTransformer.h"\n'
     '#include "scripting/ScriptEngineProvider.h"')
PY

# --- the subject: the deletion is only half done ---------------------------
#
# A translation unit named by the build but absent from the tree is not a
# completed deletion, it is a configure that cannot run. The row claims the
# unit went; this is the half that would be left behind by deleting the files
# and forgetting the list.

mutation_case "the build list names a translation unit that is gone" <<'PY'
edit("sce/sce_base_sources.cmake",
     "    src/scripting/SessionRegistry.cpp\n",
     "    src/scripting/SessionRegistry.cpp\n"
     "    src/scripting/EcmaScriptToLuaTransformer.cpp\n")
PY

# --- the gate: a sweep that stopped reading --------------------------------
#
# "No file names X" is the answer a sweep gives when it read nothing, and with
# the unit deleted that answer is also the row's claim. The floors are what
# tell them apart: a population filter that matches nothing, or one narrowed
# back to the two directories the sweep used to read, is how it would come to
# read nothing while still reporting success.

mutation_case "the engine sweep is pointed at a directory that does not exist" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/lowering_decision_ledger.rs",
     'p.starts_with("sce/src/") || p.starts_with("sce/include/")',
     'p.starts_with("sce/srcs/") || p.starts_with("sce/includes/")')
PY

mutation_case "the sweep narrows back to the engine's own directories" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/lowering_decision_ledger.rs",
     '            .filter(|p| is_cpp_source(p))\n'
     '            .collect();\n'
     '        assert!(\n'
     '            population.len() >= TRACKED_CPP_FLOOR,',
     '            .filter(|p| is_cpp_source(p) && p.starts_with("sce/"))\n'
     '            .collect();\n'
     '        assert!(\n'
     '            population.len() >= TRACKED_CPP_FLOOR,')
PY

# ⚠ The extension filter is the third way this population can empty, and until
# 2026-08-30 only the KOTLIN half had a case for it — found while re-aiming
# that one. The two halves have the same shape (`is_cpp_source` against
# `TRACKED_CPP_FLOOR`, `is_kotlin_source` against `TRACKED_KOTLIN_FLOOR`), so a
# guard exercised on one side and not the other is a gap rather than a
# judgement about C++. Anchored on the fragment `rustfmt` keeps on one line,
# for the reason the Kotlin case now spells out.
mutation_case "the C++ sweep is pointed at an extension nothing carries" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/lowering_decision_ledger.rs",
     '        Some("cpp" | "cc" | "cxx" | "h" | "hpp" | "c")\n',
     '        Some("cppx" | "ccx" | "cxxx" | "hx" | "hppx" | "cx")\n')
PY

# ⚠⚠⚠ The one that matters most. The mention control is the ONLY thing left
# standing between "no file reaches the rewriter" and "no file was read", now
# that the unit whose own files used to prove the predicate can see is gone.
# Blind the control and the claim keeps reporting success over an unread tree.

mutation_case "the control that proves the sweep reads goes blind" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/lowering_decision_ledger.rs",
     '            .filter(|p| read(p).contains(REWRITER))\n'
     '            .collect();\n'
     '        assert!(\n'
     '            mentions.len() >= REWRITER_MENTION_FLOOR,',
     '            .filter(|_p| false)\n'
     '            .collect();\n'
     '        assert!(\n'
     '            mentions.len() >= REWRITER_MENTION_FLOOR,')
PY

mutation_case "an include with no call stops counting as reaching it" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/lowering_decision_ledger.rs",
     '    let raw: Vec<&str> = src.lines().collect();\n'
     '    code.lines().enumerate().any(|(n, line)| {',
     '    let raw: Vec<&str> = src.lines().collect();\n'
     '    let _ = &raw;\n'
     '    return false;\n'
     '    #[allow(unreachable_code)]\n'
     '    code.lines().enumerate().any(|(n, line)| {')
PY

# --- the gate: the pin under a retired measurement -------------------------
#
# A number whose subject has left the tree is re-derivable only if some commit
# still holds the subject. Each case here breaks one leg of that: the commit,
# the departure, and the probe that says which commit to go to.

# ⚠⚠⚠ The pin arm cannot run in a shallow clone, and CI clones shallow by
# default — measured 2026-08-29, when the first form of this check went red on
# the two lanes that clone shallow (`tree-hygiene`, and `mutation-rounds`,
# where it read as `baseline is not green (1 failing)` rather than as itself)
# for a tree that was correct. The miss is now CLASSIFIED (the
# checkout must PROVE it is shallow) rather than read as "the commit is gone",
# and the cases below are what stop that classification from becoming an
# exemption. `the pin names a commit this repository does not carry` runs
# where clones are full, so a fallback made unconditional would let a bogus
# sha through and show up there.
#
# ⚠⚠⚠⚠ The other three take away the PRECONDITION, one hop each. The lane
# where the arm is real is not a name in the check, it is derived: a gate
# script says which slug runs this test, a workflow job says which slug it
# runs, and the job says how it checks out. A case per hop, because a
# derivation is exactly as strong as its weakest one and a hop nothing breaks
# is a hop nobody is verifying.

mutation_case "the lane that verifies the pin stops fetching history" <<'PY'
edit(".github/workflows/tree-hygiene.yml",
     "      with:\n"
     "        fetch-depth: 0\n",
     "")
PY

mutation_case "the lane stops running the gate that runs this test" <<'PY'
edit(".github/workflows/tree-hygiene.yml",
     "      run: scripts/gate tree-hygiene\n",
     "      run: true  # gate removed\n")
PY

mutation_case "the gate that owns this test stops selecting it" <<'PY'
edit("scripts/gates/tree-hygiene.sh",
     "    --test lowering_decision_ledger \\\n",
     "")
PY

mutation_case "the pin names a commit this repository does not carry" <<'PY'
edit("docs/SCE_LUA_TRANSLATION_SEAM.md",
     '`retired-measurement:59eb7f96022fa4a10330fbfd70c05b45671af443` | `scripts/measure-lowering-per-call.sh`',
     '`retired-measurement:0000000000000000000000000000000000000000` | `scripts/measure-lowering-per-call.sh`')
PY

mutation_case "a departed path is claimed for a file that is still here" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/lowering_decision_ledger.rs",
     '    "tests/benchmarks/EcmaLoweringPerCallBenchmark.cpp",',
     '    "sce/src/scripting/LuaEngine.cpp",')
PY

mutation_case "the retired probe stops saying that it is retired" <<'PY'
edit("scripts/measure-lowering-per-call.sh",
     "# `retired-measurement:<sha>`, and sce-build/tests/lowering_decision_ledger.rs",
     "# a pin recorded elsewhere, and sce-build/tests/lowering_decision_ledger.rs")
PY

# --- the KOTLIN half: the same claim, one language over --------------------
#
# `kotlin-retire-rewriter` closed on 2026-08-30, four rounds after its C++
# sibling, and it is a SEPARATE row over a SEPARATE population for the reason
# these cases are separate too: one sweep over both backends would have
# reported success here from the day C++ deleted its half, because "no file
# reaches the rewriter" is satisfied by a population that does not contain the
# file that does.
#
# The subject cases are written as REVERTS. The member and the import are how
# the engine and its suite actually stood, so a careless revert reproduces
# them — and with the unit deleted neither compiles, which is the point.

mutation_case "the Kotlin engine takes the rewriter back as a member" <<'PY'
edit("backends/kotlin/lua/src/main/kotlin/com/sce/scripting/lua/LuaScriptEngine.kt",
     "    private val sessions = mutableMapOf<String, Session>()\n",
     "    private val sessions = mutableMapOf<String, Session>()\n"
     "    private val transformer = EcmaScriptToLuaTransformer()\n")
PY

mutation_case "a Kotlin suite imports the retired unit again" <<'PY'
edit("backends/kotlin/tests/src/test/kotlin/com/sce/ecmascript/LoweredEcma262Test.kt",
     "import com.sce.scripting.lua.LuaScriptEngine\n",
     "import com.sce.scripting.lua.EcmaScriptToLuaTransformer\n"
     "import com.sce.scripting.lua.LuaScriptEngine\n")
PY

# --- the gate: the Kotlin sweep goes blind ---------------------------------
#
# "No file names X" is what a sweep answers when it read nothing, and with the
# unit deleted that is also the row's claim. The population filter, the floor
# and the mention control are the three ways it can come to read nothing while
# still reporting success.

# ⚠ RE-AIMED 2026-08-30, and the reason is worth more than the repair. The
# first form quoted the whole `matches!(…)` on ONE line. That broke no rule
# this repository had written down — it is the case's own subject, not an
# argument list — and it was stale the moment it landed, because `rustfmt`
# wraps that call across three lines and a one-line quotation matches no byte
# sequence in the file. `mutation-cases` reported it as INCONCLUSIVE, which
# would have read as SURVIVED on any round that did not look.
#
# ▶ The general form to carry: an anchor in Rust must be a fragment `rustfmt`
# KEEPS on one line. `Some("kt" | "kts")` is that fragment here, it occurs
# exactly once in the file, and the extension set is what the case is aiming
# at anyway.
mutation_case "the Kotlin sweep is pointed at an extension nothing carries" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/lowering_decision_ledger.rs",
     '        Some("kt" | "kts")\n',
     '        Some("ktx" | "ktsx")\n')
PY

mutation_case "the control that proves the Kotlin sweep reads goes blind" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/lowering_decision_ledger.rs",
     '            .filter(|p| read(p).contains(REWRITER))\n'
     '            .collect();\n'
     '        assert!(\n'
     '            mentions.len() >= KOTLIN_MENTION_FLOOR,',
     '            .filter(|_p| false)\n'
     '            .collect();\n'
     '        assert!(\n'
     '            mentions.len() >= KOTLIN_MENTION_FLOOR,')
PY

# --- the gate: the Kotlin reader stops telling prose from code -------------
#
# The whole weight of the claim rests on this distinction: four Kotlin files
# still explain the rewriter in comments, and they are the sweep's only
# remaining proof that it can see the name at all. A reader that counted those
# as callers would fail a correct tree; one that leaked a raw string's body, or
# closed a nested comment early, would do the same in the other direction.

# ⚠ The call is KEPT and its result discarded, rather than replaced. Dropping
# it outright leaves `kotlin_code_only` with no caller, and this crate denies
# dead code: the mutation then does not compile and the round reports
# INCONCLUSIVE — a verdict that looks like a surviving mutation and is not one.
# Measured 2026-08-30, on this very case.
mutation_case "the Kotlin reader stops stripping comments" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/lowering_decision_ledger.rs",
     "fn reaches_rewriter_kt(src: &str) -> bool {\n"
     "    kotlin_code_only(src).contains(REWRITER)",
     "fn reaches_rewriter_kt(src: &str) -> bool {\n"
     "    let _ = kotlin_code_only(src);\n"
     "    src.contains(REWRITER)")
PY

# ⚠ Both of the cases below SURVIVED their first form, and neither was a hole
# in the gate — the FIXTURES could not observe them. A raw string whose body
# held an even number of quotes is consumed identically by the ordinary-string
# reader, and a nested comment with the name inside the INNER comment is
# consumed on the way to the first terminator either way. The repair was to
# move the subject, not to strengthen the assertion: a fixture asks the
# question its shape decides.
mutation_case "a raw string leaks its body into the code side" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/lowering_decision_ledger.rs",
     '        if c == \'"\' && bytes.get(i + 1) == Some(&\'"\') && bytes.get(i + 2) == Some(&\'"\') {\n'
     '            out.push_str("\\"\\"\\"");',
     '        if false {\n'
     '            out.push_str("\\"\\"\\"");')
PY

mutation_case "a nested block comment closes at the first terminator" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/tests/lowering_decision_ledger.rs",
     "                if bytes[i] == '/' && bytes.get(i + 1) == Some(&'*') {\n"
     "                    depth += 1;\n"
     "                    i += 2;\n"
     "                    continue;\n"
     "                }\n",
     "")
PY
